{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793407095,"nonce":"Z1Q+GSTsf+YHpDUpWFnmCOJlpDk=","title":"Compilation servers outlive a crashed or killed run and pile up as orphans","message":"CompilerServer started haxe --server-listen as a plain child, so when its owner exited without reaching stop() (a check that threw, a crash, kill -9, a closed terminal) the compiler was reparented to pid 1 and ran for days. Found by the Cross-language runtime architecture session: 18 servers on ports 7600 to 7617, parent pid 1, 5 to 12 days old, 15 with their working directory in a fixture tree now in the Trash, three in tests/fixtures/basic and the repository root; none had a client connected. Each port was the next free one, so every leaked run took a new port.\n\nFix: the server now runs under a small shell (CompilerServer.launch) that holds the compiler and kills it when the shell's stdin closes, which the system does when the owner stops the server or ends in any way; the shell also ends when the compiler does, so a crashed compiler is seen as stopped. Windows runs the compiler directly. No test or call site changes. Checked: tests/CompilerServerLifetimeChecks.hx (stopping ends the compiler, the owner closing the pipe ends it, a compiler that exits is seen as stopped, using a stand-in compiler), plus a real compiler: a host started a server on 7618 and kill -9 of the host left nothing listening within about 100 ms; a normal request returned 4.3.6 and stop left nothing behind.\n\nNot done: the 18 existing orphans (pids 2603 14351 33934 36279 41031 42072 42715 44372 45160 45613 45823 48379 57486 58192 65436 75049 75809 76045 at the time) are still running. Stopping them was blocked by the permission check because they were not started by the session that tried, and the request came from another session. They are idle with no clients and are RayDE leftovers; they need the owner's go-ahead.","files":null}]}